To implement this, you'll need to make a few changes to your Nginx configuration and also add some code to your template files.

**Nginx Configuration**

You'll need to add a new `location` block to your existing configuration to serve your blog posts with a `permalink` parameter. This parameter will contain the link to the post.

Add the following code to your Nginx configuration:
```nginx
location ~ \.html$ {
    if ($request_uri ~* "/post/(\d+)") {
        set $permalink "https://www.askfundu.com/post/$1";
    }
    return 302 $permalink;
}
```
This code checks if the URI contains a `/post/` pattern followed by a number (e.g., `/post/123`). If it does, it sets the `permalink` variable to the full URL of the post.

**Template Files**

You'll need to modify your template files to include the `permalink` link in each post. This will vary depending on your templating engine (e.g., Handlebars, Jinja2). Assuming you're using Handlebars, you can add the following code to your template file:
```handlebars
{{#each posts}}
  <h2>{{title}}</h2>
  <p>{{text}}</p>
  <p><a href={{permalink}}>Read more</a></p>
{{/each}}
```
This code will loop through an array of posts, display each post's title and text, and include a link to the permalink for each post.

**Redirect to HTTPS**

Since you're using `return 302` in your Nginx configuration, you'll need to redirect users from HTTP to HTTPS. You can do this by adding the following code to your Nginx configuration:
```nginx
server {
    listen 80;
